.TH EUCLID-CLI-ESM-SET-BUCKET-LIFECYCLE 1 "October 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-esm-set-bucket-lifecycle, get-bucket-lifecycle, delete-bucket-lifecycle \- the rules that archive a bucket's unused objects
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli esm set-bucket-lifecycle
.BR \-\-bucket | \-b
.I NAME|ERN
.BR \-\-rule | \-r
.I RULE
\&...
.br
.B euclid-cli esm get-bucket-lifecycle
.BR \-\-bucket | \-b
.I NAME|ERN
.br
.B euclid-cli esm delete-bucket-lifecycle
.BR \-\-bucket | \-b
.I NAME|ERN
.SH DESCRIPTION
An object is in one of two lifecycle states.
.B STANDARD
is online: it reads as it always has.
.B ARCHIVED
is offline: its bytes have been moved into a zip in
.B euclid.modules.esm.archive-dir
(which may be a cheaper disk), and
.BR get-object ,
.BR create-download ,
.B copy-object
and
.B move-object
refuse it with 409 until it is restored with
.BR "esm restore-object" .
Its key, attributes, size and listing entry stay where they were, and it can be
deleted or written over as before; writing over it puts the new bytes online.
.PP
Archives are ordinary zip files (Zip64), one per bucket and month, appended to as
objects are archived:
.IR archive-dir / account / namespace /archive- bucket - MMYYYY .zip,
for example
.BR 000000000000/development/archive-file-delivery-012026.zip .
Each member is named by the object's key and deflated unless its bytes are
encrypted or already compressed;
.B unzip
or any other zip tool opens them. A restore copies the member back out and leaves
it in the zip. A zip that no object is archived in any more \- everything in it
restored, deleted or overwritten \- is deleted by the lifecycle sweep once it
has not been written to for a day.
.PP
.B set-bucket-lifecycle
replaces a bucket's rules with the ones given. A rule archives every object
under its prefix (the whole bucket without one) that has not been used for
.I after
days \- used meaning read, written, copied or moved, as
.B lastAccessed
in
.B "esm list-objects"
shows it. Where several rules match a key, the one with the longest prefix
decides. Invalid rules are refused as a whole: a bucket never ends up with half
of a set.
.PP
A restore keeps an object online for the rule's
.I restore
days, the grace period. If nobody reads it in that time it is archived again at
the end of it; if somebody does, the rule counts from that read, as for any
other object.
.PP
The rules are applied by a background sweep. A pass over every bucket with
rules starts at most every
.B euclid.modules.esm.lifecycle-sweep-period
seconds (an hour by default), works for up to
.B euclid.modules.esm.lifecycle-sweep-budget
seconds (240) per run, and carries on from where it stopped every five minutes
until it is done. A new rule therefore takes effect within about the period,
plus however long a pass over the bucket takes.
.PP
.B delete-bucket-lifecycle
removes all of a bucket's rules. Nothing more is archived; what is archived
already stays archived.
.PP
Euclid's internal buckets cannot have rules: the modules that read them read
their files directly.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bucket " " \fINAME|ERN\fR
The bucket, by name or ERN.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-rule " " \fIRULE\fR
One rule, repeatable:
.BI id= ID ,after= DAYS ,restore= DAYS
optionally followed by
.BI ,prefix= PREFIX
and
.BR ,disabled .
Both day counts are from 1 to 36500; ids are unique within the bucket.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli esm set-bucket-lifecycle -b incoming \e
    --rule id=cold,after=90,restore=7 \e
    --rule id=logs,prefix=logs/,after=14,restore=2
euclid-cli esm get-bucket-lifecycle -b incoming
euclid-cli esm delete-bucket-lifecycle -b incoming
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The rules were set, shown or removed.
.TP
.B 1
No such bucket, an invalid rule, not permitted, or invalid arguments.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-esm-archive-object (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1)
